mill-proof.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps mill-proof.logic.r2.2 and mill-proof.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "we have all the proof which the case admits of that happiness is a good", "each person's happiness is a good to that person" and "the general happiness is a good to the aggregate of all persons" are true and "each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness" is false. Is S6, "This, however, being a fact, we have not only all the proof which the case admits of, but all which it is possible to require, that happiness is a good: that each person's happiness is a good to that person, and the general happiness, therefore, a good to the aggregate of all persons.", true then?
